description:
Deterministic island heightmap: seedable PRNG, coarse grid (PS1-era triangle counts), beach falloff so edges drop below sea level. Expose height_at(x,z) and normal_at(x,z) sampling for gameplay and camera.

## Design
src/terrain.c/h. Grid: 96x96 cells over 192x192 m (~18k tris — PS1-era count).

Deterministic value noise, no RNG state to serialize:

    static uint32_t hash2(int x, int z, uint32_t seed) {
        uint32_t h = seed ^ (uint32_t)x * 0x9E3779B1u ^ (uint32_t)z * 0x85EBCA77u;
        h = (h ^ (h >> 13)) * 0xC2B2AE35u;
        return h ^ (h >> 16);
    }
    // value(x,z) = hash -> [0,1); bilinear between lattice points
    // fbm = v(x,z) * 0.7 + v(x*2, z*2) * 0.3   (2 octaves)

Height:

    float h = (fbm - 0.5f) * 2.0f * AMP;            // AMP ~ 26 m
    float r = length(x, z) / (HALF * 0.85f);        // radial, smoothed corners ok
    h -= smoothstepf(0.75f, 1.15f, r) * DROP;       // DROP ~ 30 m -> rim below 0

Precompute into `float H[96][96]` at init (vertices snap to grid points — the faceted look wants that, not smooth interpolation):

    void  terrain_init(Terrain* t, uint32_t seed);
    float terrain_height_at(const Terrain* t, float x, float z);  // nearest grid sample
    void  terrain_normal_at(const Terrain* t, float x, float z, vec3 out);  // central-difference across the cell, normalized

The mesh task (island1-xf8m) picks grass/sand/rock per vertex from height + slope; props (island1-mvpl) sample the same functions.

## Done when
Same seed -> identical island across runs/platforms; island sits mostly above SEA_LEVEL=0 with a below-water rim forming a beach ring (island1-o116); normals are ~unit length; height_at is fast enough for per-tick collision (island1-md2b).

Car-like hover physics: position, heading, velocity, throttle/brake, speed-dependent steering, lateral grip that can be exceeded to produce drift, yaw rate from steering * speed, water drag vs land drag. Runs on the fixed timestep.

## Design
src/car.c/h. World units: meters, tick = 1/60s.

    typedef struct {
        vec3  pos, vel;      // world space
        float heading;       // yaw, radians, +Z forward
        float yaw_rate;      // smoothed
        float speed_kmh;     // for HUD
        bool  on_water;
    } CarState;

Tick body:

    // 1. control axes from input: throttle, brake in [0,1]; steer in {-1,0,1}
    vec3 fwd   = { sinf(h), 0, cosf(h) };
    vec3 right = { fwd[2], 0, -fwd[0] };
    float v_f = glm_vec3_dot(vel, fwd), v_r = glm_vec3_dot(vel, right);

    // 2. longitudinal
    v_f += (throttle*ACCEL - brake*BRAKE) * dt;
    v_f -= DRAG*(on_water ? WATER_K : LAND_K) * v_f * dt;   // water drag higher
    v_f = clampf(v_f, -MAX_REV, MAX_FWD);

    // 3. lateral grip — the drift knob
    float grip = minf(1.0f, GRIP * dt);
    v_r -= v_r * grip;
    // reduce grip under hard steering at speed -> slide:
    float drift = minf(1.0f, fabsf(steer) * fabsf(v_f) / 18.0f);
    v_r -= v_r * minf(1.0f, grip * (1.0f - 0.7f * drift));  // keep more lateral when drifting

    // 4. yaw: needs speed to turn, smoothed rate
    float yaw_target = steer * YAW_GAIN * clampf(fabsf(v_f) / 8.0f, 0.0f, 1.0f);
    yaw_rate += (yaw_target - yaw_rate) * minf(1.0f, YAW_SMOOTH * dt);
    heading += yaw_rate * dt;

    // 5. recombine + integrate, then collision (island1-md2b) clamps pos and scrubs v_r on hits
    vel = fwd*v_f + right*v_r;  pos += vel*dt;

Tune constants as #defines in car.h (start: ACCEL=9, BRAKE=14, WATER_K=0.9, LAND_K=0.35, GRIP=6, YAW_GAIN=1.9, YAW_SMOOTH=5, MAX_FWD=28 m/s, MAX_REV=8).

## Done when
0-to-top in ~3.5s on water; holds a straight line with no steer input; hard steer above ~40% speed produces visible slide (v_r non-zero, decaying); reversing works; on_water flag flips at sea-level crossings.

Chase camera replacing the free-fly cam: position = car pos + offset rotated by (heading + lag), critically-damped spring follow, look-at point slightly ahead of the car, speed-based FOV widening and pull-back. Feed from the frame loop.

## Design
src/chase_cam.c/h. Keep the fly cam (camera.c) alive on F for dev flight.

    typedef struct {
        vec3 pos, vel;        // spring state
        float heading_lag;    // eased car heading, radians
    } ChaseCam;

Per render frame (dt = frame dt, not tick):

    // 1. lag the heading — the signature "camera catches up" PS1 feel
    float dh = angle_wrap(car.heading - c->heading_lag);
    c->heading_lag += dh * (1.0f - expf(-6.0f * dt));

    // 2. target point behind the car, growing with speed
    vec3 fwd = { sinf(c->heading_lag), 0, cosf(c->heading_lag) };
    float s01 = clampf(car.speed_kmh / 100.0f, 0.0f, 1.0f);
    float dist   = 7.0f + s01 * 4.0f;     // pull-back
    float height = 3.2f + s01 * 1.2f;
    vec3 target = { car.pos[0] - fwd[0]*dist, car.pos[1] + height, car.pos[2] - fwd[2]*dist };

    // 3. critically-damped spring (stable at dt <= 1/60)
    float w = 8.0f;
    vec3 acc = (target - c->pos) * w * w - 2.0f * w * c->vel;
    c->vel += acc * dt;  c->pos += c->vel * dt;
    // 4. above-terrain clamp (full version: island1-qvn1)
    if (c->pos[1] < terrain_height_at(...)+1.5f) c->pos[1] = terrain_height_at(...)+1.5f;

    look_at = car.pos + fwd * 4.0f + (0, 0.8, 0);
    fov = 58.0f + s01 * 8.0f;   // feed cam_update() / glm_perspective

## Done when
No jitter at constant speed; hard turn shows a visible ~0.3s heading lag then settles (no overshoot beyond a few cm at w=8); at top speed the camera pulls back and FOV widens; terrain clamp keeps the camera above the island on hill crests.

New input.c/h: named actions (throttle, steer, brake, boost, pickup, deliver, look), per-frame edge detection (pressed/released) plus held state, mouse delta accumulation. Move handle_input() out of main.c onto this.

## Design
src/input.h:

    typedef enum {
        ACT_THROTTLE, ACT_BRAKE, ACT_STEER_L, ACT_STEER_R, ACT_BOOST,
        ACT_PICKUP, ACT_DELIVER, ACT_LOOK, ACT_QUIT, ACT_COUNT
    } Action;

    typedef struct {
        uint8_t held[ACT_COUNT];
        uint8_t pressed[ACT_COUNT];   // rising edge this tick
        uint8_t released[ACT_COUNT];  // falling edge this tick
        float   mouse_dx, mouse_dy;   // accumulated since last begin_frame
    } InputState;

    void            input_init(GLFWwindow* w);   // registers all GLFW callbacks
    void            input_begin_frame(void);     // called once per logic tick: clears edges, zeroes mouse delta
    void            input_poll(void);            // folds pending events into state
    const InputState* input_state(void);
    bool             input_held(Action a);
    bool             input_pressed(Action a);

- GLFW callbacks only write into a pending-flags array (atomic-ish booleans); input_poll() folds them into InputState so each fixed tick sees a consistent snapshot.
- Default bindings: W throttle, S brake/reverse, A/D steer, SHIFT boost, E context action (pickup/deliver), RMB look, ESC quit.
- Removes g_rmb_down, g_last_mouse_x/y and handle_input() from src/main.c (src/main.c:29-102); fly-cam look reads input mouse delta instead.

## Done when
Holding E gives pressed()==true on exactly one tick then held()==true; mouse delta is consumed once per tick; main.c contains no GLFW key queries.

Replace ad-hoc main.c frame handling with an accumulator loop: 60Hz logic tick, render as fast as vsync allows, clamped dt, frame counter used by physics/animation.

## Design
Fixed tick `TICK = 1.0/60.0`; cap accumulator backlog at 5 ticks to avoid the spiral of death. Expose `uint64_t g_frame_no` and `double g_time` to subsystems.

Replace the while-loop in src/main.c:181-201 with:

    double last = glfwGetTime();
    float acc = 0.0f;
    while (!glfwWindowShouldClose(g_window)) {
        double now = glfwGetTime();
        float frame_dt = (float)(now - last); last = now;
        if (frame_dt > 0.25f) frame_dt = 0.25f;   // clamp hiccups
        acc += frame_dt;
        int ticks = 0;
        while (acc >= TICK && ticks < 5) {
            input_begin_frame();      // snapshot input for this tick
            car_step(&g_car, &input_state, TICK);
            delivery_step(&g_deliv, &g_car, TICK);
            acc -= TICK; ticks++;
        }
        if (ticks == 5) acc = 0.0f;   // drop the backlog
        render(accumulator_alpha = acc / TICK);  // alpha for future interpolation
        glfwSwapBuffers(g_window);
        glfwPollEvents();
    }

## Details
- Step order per tick: input snapshot -> car physics -> delivery logic.
- Mouse-look (RMB drag) stays per-event; the chase camera integrates its spring in render with frame dt (see island1-fnss).
- Render must still run when zero ticks execute (minimized window, high refresh).
- Verification: hold W and read out speed — constant regardless of refresh rate; log tick rate to confirm ~60Hz.

## Done when
main.c loop matches the sketch, `ticks==5` path is exercised (temporarily set TICK=0.05f in a test), and no subsystem reads glfw time directly.
